## ABSTRACT

Global atmospheric CO₂ has surpassed 422 ppm. Combined natural sink and industrial capture capacity stands at roughly 50 Mt yr⁻¹ — under 0.14 % of annual emissions of ~37 Gt yr⁻¹. The Solar Synthetic Tree (SST) is a new category of distributed climate infrastructure that simultaneously captures CO₂ from ambient air, generates renewable electricity, electrochemically converts CO₂ into stable carbon compounds or value-added chemicals, permanently mineralises the remainder underground, harvests atmospheric water, cools the urban microclimate, and serves as a culturally adaptive public installation — all governed by a hierarchical artificial intelligence system with a domain-fine-tuned large language model at its apex.

This PhD project is conceived as a purely theoretical and computational endeavour, entirely executable by an independent researcher without a physical laboratory. It establishes the complete scientific, mathematical, and architectural framework for the SST v4.0. The work integrates six 2025–2026 validated scientific pillars:

1. Flexible perovskite-silicon tandem photovoltaics now certified at **33.6 %** efficiency (Wang et al., *Nature* 649, 2026), and rigid tandems at **32.89 %** (NIMTE/Soochow/Matter, May 2026), with the absolute cell record at **34.85 %** (LONGi, NREL-certified, April 2025) — all exceeding the Shockley-Queisser single-junction silicon limit of 33.7 %;
2. Nobel-winning metal-organic frameworks (Yaghi, 2025) merged with electrically conductive redox-active MOF architectures — specifically Ni₃(HITP)₂ with embedded Ni-BDI units (JACS, 2025) and Cu₃(HHTP)₂ (RSC *J. Mater. Chem. A*, 2026) — achieving **96 % capacity utilisation and 98 % Faradaic efficiency** over 50 electrochemical capture-release cycles;
3. Direct Air Electrowinning (DAE) — the integration of alkaline air capture with direct bicarbonate electrolysis in a single zero-gap flow cell, now with multiple 2026 peer-reviewed demonstrations (Delft/ACS Sustainable Chem. Eng., 2026; Phlair Electra operational plant, Rotterdam) and a solid-electrolyte reactor achieving DAC-to-ethylene at a cell voltage as low as **0.6 V**;
4. Hybrid olivine–cement kiln dust mineralisation for rapid and permanent geological carbon storage, with an optional precipitated calcium carbonate (PCC) revenue stream;
5. A three-layer AI control stack: Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) at the edge node, federated LSTM-based anomaly detection across nodes, and a domain-fine-tuned open-weight large language model (Llama 4 or equivalent) as strategic orchestrator, regulatory engine, and multilingual citizen interface;
6. Full alignment with the EU Carbon Removal Certification Framework (CRCF, Regulation EU/2024/3012), whose first delegated act establishing DACCS certification methodology was adopted in **February 2026**, with certification bodies accepting project applications from April 2026.

The proposal defines three falsifiable, simultaneous success criteria:

- A single SST unit achieves net-carbon-negative operation (CO₂ captured exceeds embodied manufacturing CO₂) within ≤ 4 years of simulated desert operation, verified by independent third-party lifecycle assessment;
- An LLM-orchestrated fleet of 100 simulated nodes captures ≥ 10 % more CO₂ per unit energy than a rule-based control fleet under identical climate forcing;
- The levelised cost of carbon removal (LCCR) is ≤ €180 t⁻¹, competitive with 2026 commercial electrochemical DAC, while delivering quantifiable co-benefits in cooling, water, and biodiversity.

The PhD delivers an open-source SST Digital Twin v4.0 and domain-fine-tuned LLM weights, providing a computationally verified blueprint ready for future physical prototyping.

