## 1. INTRODUCTION AND MOTIVATION

### 1.1 The Carbon Removal Gap

Global CO₂ emissions remain at approximately 37 Gt yr⁻¹. As of 2026, the totality of operational CO₂ capture and storage capacity amounts to roughly 50 Mt yr⁻¹ — barely 0.14 % of annual emissions. The carbon removal market, estimated at USD 2.7 billion in 2023, is projected to grow toward USD 100 billion per year by 2030–2035, driven by over 600 CCUS projects in various pipeline stages and approximately 15 % year-on-year growth in project activity. The EU Carbon Removal Certification Framework (CRCF, Regulation EU/2024/3012), which entered force in December 2024 and launched its first DACCS certification methodology in February 2026, is now creating the regulatory and economic architecture into which the SST must be designed. The State of Carbon Dioxide Removal 2024 estimates only 1.3 Mt yr⁻¹ of novel CDR today, against 7–9 Gt yr⁻¹ required by mid-century — a 5,000-fold scale-up gap.

### 1.2 The Vision: Infrastructure as Regenerative Ecology

Existing DAC plants are large, centralised industrial facilities. The SST proposes a radically different paradigm: distributed, multifunctional, biomimetic nodes that weave carbon removal, energy generation, water harvesting, microclimate cooling, biodiversity monitoring, and public art into the urban fabric. Each SST unit is an autonomous climate-positive organism linked into a federated intelligence network.

Unlike Klaus Lackner's Mechanical Tree — which uses passive sorbent tiles for CO₂ absorption alone — the SST captures, electrochemically converts, and permanently mineralises CO₂, while delivering a full suite of urban ecosystem services. The SST's LLM orchestration layer enables natural-language citizen interfaces and automated participation in CRCF-certified carbon credit markets — capabilities no single-purpose DAC unit can match.

### 1.3 Nature of This PhD

This PhD is submitted by an independent researcher without laboratory access. The entire project is built on theoretical analysis, multi-physics modelling, digital twin simulation, and AI/LLM system design. Every sub-system is validated against the most recent high-quality experimental data from the literature (2025–2026). The deliverable is a computationally proven, open-source blueprint — the SST Digital Twin v4.0 — demonstrating net-carbon-negative operation *in silico*.

**Central Research Question:** Can a distributed array of SST units, powered entirely by their own solar canopies and governed by an LLM-augmented federated AI, achieve verifiable net-carbon-negative operation over a 20-year lifecycle, as demonstrated by high-fidelity multi-physics simulation and lifecycle assessment conforming to the EU CRCF QU.A.L.ITY standard?

