## 4. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE — DETAILED INNOVATION DESCRIPTION

### 4.1 Solar Canopy Layer: Energy, Water, and Cooling

**Photovoltaics:** Each "leaf" is a hermetically sealed flexible perovskite-silicon tandem module targeting **33.6 % efficiency** (Wang et al., Nature 2026). The phyllotactic canopy geometry follows the Fibonacci spiral to minimise mutual shading. The bilayer LiF/EDAI₂ interface passivation strategy (from LONGi's 34.85 % record) is evaluated in WP1 for incorporation into the tin-based lead-free variant. Halocell/Sofab metal-oxide nanoparticle transport layers provide > 1,300 h humid-heat stability. Oxford PV rigid commercial modules at 26.9 % (module, Fraunhofer-certified) serve as the engineering fallback.

**Water harvesting:** The canopy underside is patterned with an electrospun PVDF-TrFE/PAN Janus membrane achieving FCR = 42–88 mg min⁻¹ cm⁻² in coastal conditions. Conical micropores provide dual wettability gradient and Laplace pressure for spontaneous unidirectional droplet transport to the trunk reservoir. WP1 will simulate the coupled optical-hydrological performance to confirm < 2 % PV efficiency penalty from the membrane coating.

**Passive daytime radiative cooling (PDRC):** SiO₂/TiO₂/PDMS multilayer coatings (ρ_solar > 0.95, ε_LW > 0.95) on the trunk and canopy undersides. Net cooling power 40–100 W m⁻² reduces pedestrian-level air temperature by 3–8 °C beneath the SST — quantified in WP6 ENVI-met simulations.

**Tracking:** Dielectric elastomer actuator leaflet clusters consume < 3 % of generated power. The PINN optimises tilt angles in real time, balancing irradiance maximisation against passive airflow through the MOF contactor bed.

### 4.2 Central Trunk Reactor: Electro-MOF DAC + DAE Converter

**Electro-MOF Contactor:** Modular cartridges of Ni₃(HITP)₂ or Cu₃(HHTP)₂ structured as a cross-flow bed. Urban airflow (0.5–2 m s⁻¹, wind-scooped by the trunk geometry) passes through the bed; CO₂ is electrosorbed onto the conductive MOF at 0 V applied. After 90–180 min, ΔV = 0.4 V is applied across the MOF electrode for 15–30 min, releasing a CO₂-rich stream directly into the DAE reactor. No compressor, no vacuum pump, no heat source.

**DAE Reactor:** Solid-electrolyte cell (primary) with copper nanoflower cathode and IrO₂-coated Ti anode. The carbonate/bicarbonate solution from the MOF contactor feeds the cathode compartment; CO₂ and H₂O are co-reduced to ethylene at V_cell ≈ 0.6 V at j = 10–50 mA cm⁻². The liquid alkaline zero-gap flow cell (secondary, higher throughput) operates at V_cell = 2.0–2.5 V at j = 100 mA cm⁻².

**Mode switching:** The LLM Layer 3 determines which DAE mode is active based on irradiance availability (solid electrolyte preferred when P_el is limited; liquid alkaline when P_el is abundant), product storage capacity, and carbon credit market signals.

**LLM edge server:** ARM-class server (20 W peak) in a weatherproof trunk-base compartment. Runs Llama 4 / Muse Spark (INT4 quantised, 7B–13B parameters) via vLLM's PagedAttention for efficient memory management. Communicates across the cluster via 5G-mesh or LoRaWAN.

### 4.3 Artificial Root Network: Hybrid Three-Zone Mineralisation

**Zone A (top, 0–2 m depth) — CKD rapid buffer:** 50 µm CKD particles. 95 % CO₂ conversion in < 6 h. PCC harvest every 6–12 months.

**Zone B (middle, 2–10 m depth) — Olivine permanent storage:** 100 µm olivine tailings. 95 % conversion in 210–280 days. Magnesite (MgCO₃) formation provides geological-timescale permanence (> 10⁴ years). Fibre-optic distributed sensors (pH, carbonate concentration, temperature) feed the PINN in real time.

**Zone C (bottom, 10–15 m depth) — Biochar soil:** Carbon enhancement and nutrient supply. Provides the ecological foundation signal to the biodiversity sensor suite.

**Cassette management:** Full encapsulation. PHREEQC model demonstrates WHO-compliant leachate in worst-case breach scenario. Cassettes are replaced by a local maintenance crew every 5–7 years, with the spent cassette containing verified, mineralised carbon contributing directly to the CRCF MRV record.

### 4.4 Intelligent Network Layer: Tri-Level AI in Physical Deployment

**Layer 1 (per-node):** NVIDIA Jetson Orin (< 15 W). Sub-10 ms control loop. Hardware interlock physically prevents remote override of safety-critical actuator limits.

**Layer 2 (cluster-wide):** TensorFlow Federated across all cluster nodes via LoRaWAN/5G-mesh. Differential privacy (ε = 1.0, δ = 10⁻⁵) enforced per training round. Only gradient updates shared — raw sensor data never leaves the node.

**Layer 3 (cluster hub):** vLLM-hosted quantised LLM on a low-power ARM edge server. Internet-connected for weather API, carbon registry, and CRCF submission. All safety-critical control paths are physically air-gapped from the LLM inference path.

**Cybersecurity:** Hyperledger Fabric blockchain audit trail records every carbon capture event and every LLM strategic decision. Constitutional AI filter prevents prompt injection from the citizen mobile interface from reaching the actuator control path.

