Appendix B: Conductive Electro-MOF Direct Air Capture

B.1 Ni₃(HITP)₂ with Ni-BDI Units (JACS, 2025)

Meng et al. (2025) reported the first implementation of electrically conductive metal-organic framework (MOF) Ni₃(HITP)₂ integrated with nickel bis(diimine) (Ni-BDI) units for solid-state electrochemical CO₂ capture. The system achieved 96% capacity utilisation and 98% Faradaic efficiency over 50 capture-release cycles without capacity degradation. Critically, the material demonstrated resistance to O₂, H₂O, NO₂, and SO₂ interference — the essential real-world durability attribute for urban air deployment . Electrochemical regeneration involves potential control of the Ni centre's redox state, driving reversible carbamate formation and release via a one-electron reaction per Ni site. The estimated regeneration energy is approximately 39.8 kJ mol⁻¹ CO₂ (ΔV_redox ≈ 0.4 V, η_Faradaic = 0.97), representing a ~6-fold reduction relative to thermal swing DAC regeneration (230–800 kJ mol⁻¹ thermal equivalent).

B.2 Cu₃(HHTP)₂ (RSC J. Mater. Chem. A, 2026)

Copper-2,3,6,7,10,11-hexahydroxytriphenylene (Cu₃(HHTP)₂) was demonstrated as the first MOF to switch its ability to capture and release CO₂ in aqueous electrolytes via reversible electrosorption, confirmed by cyclic voltammetry, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy, galvanostatic charge-discharge analysis, and differential electrochemical mass spectrometry (DEMS) . DFT calculations confirm that efficient CO₂ capture is mediated by the combination of the redox-active copper atom and the aromatic HHTP ligand. This provides a copper-based alternative to the nickel system, with distinct voltage-window and kinetic characteristics .

B.3 Farha Group Computational Screening (JACS, 2025)

Farha, Chen et al. (2025) published a comprehensive perspective in JACS examining molecular-level effects of water vapour on trace CO₂ capture and reviewing data-driven computational screening methods. Their LVGP-MOBBO (Latent Variable Gaussian Process and Multi-Objective Batch-Bayesian Optimisation) pipeline identified all Pareto-optimal MOFs for DAC by searching only ~1% of the design space. The framework provides the precise computational screening protocol adopted in SST WP1 .

B.4 Electrochemical Capture Review (2025)

Castro-Castillo et al. (2025) published a comprehensive review of redox-active MOFs as electrosorbent materials for atmospheric CO₂ capture, highlighting the transformative nature of electrochemical capture — fully reversible operation under mild conditions with direct renewable energy compatibility .

Appendix C: Direct Air Electrowinning (DAE) — 2024–2026 Demonstrations

C.1 Phlair Electra Operational Plant (Rotterdam)

Phlair (formerly Carbon Atlantis) secured €14.5 million in September 2024 to develop two 260 t-CO₂ yr⁻¹ FOAK (First-of-a-Kind) plants, Electra 01 and Electra 02, using behind-the-meter solar power. The Hydrolyzer technology operates at approximately 200 kWh t⁻¹ CO₂, claimed to use up to 3× less energy than thermal DAC . Captured CO₂ is permanently mineralised via the Phlair-Paebbl partnership. Forward credits from Electra 01 (260 tCO₂ yr⁻¹, starting 2025) were available on the CEEZER platform. Phlair plans to launch its first commercial facility, Dawn, in 2026 to remove 20 ktCO₂ yr⁻¹ . The water demand is reported at 8.2 m³ t⁻¹ CO₂ in the 260 t yr⁻¹ pilot plant, declining to 7.7 m³ t⁻¹ at 1 Mt yr⁻¹ scale .

C.2 TU Delft Dual-Sorbent Integrated DAE System (ACS Sustainable Chem. Eng., 2026)

Burgers, Kortlever et al. (2026) demonstrated a dual-sorbent integrated system in which an amine-functionalised solid sorbent captures CO₂ from air, is regenerated by rinsing with an aqueous carbonate-rich solution, and the resulting bicarbonate-rich solution is fed directly to a zero-gap electrolyser for conversion to syngas. The study identifies the pH-swing between rich and lean solutions as the dominant design parameter, governed almost exclusively by electrolyser sizing .

C.3 Solid Electrolyte Reactor for DAC-to-Ethylene at 0.6 V (Energy & Fuels, 2024)

Rice University and Chemelectronics LLC researchers demonstrated a solid electrolyte electrochemical cell that captures CO₂ and H₂O simultaneously from air and converts them to ethylene and oxygen at a full-cell voltage of only 0.6 V at 1 A current. The reactor achieves approximately 80% energy efficiency, producing ~70 mg of ethylene per hour, and operates at temperatures below 25 °C — dramatically lower than liquid alkaline systems (2.4 V, ~34% energy efficiency) .

C.4 Bicarbonate-to-Ethylene Copper Catalysis (2025)

Fu et al. (2025) demonstrated selective electrochemical ethylene production from bicarbonate solution on Cu-based catalysts with over 90% CO₂ utilisation efficiency. Optimising catalyst pore structure and copper surface oxide was identified as the key to maximising C₂H₄ production from bicarbonate solutions . A parallel study achieved ethylene Faradaic efficiency of 58.9% with a partial current density of 353.4 mA cm⁻² in KHCO₃ electrolyte .

Appendix D: Carbon Mineralisation — CarbFix, Enhanced Weathering, and Olivine Kinetics

D.1 CarbFix (Iceland)

The CarbFix technology dissolves captured CO₂ in water and injects it into basaltic rock formations at 300–1,000 m depth. CO₂ mineralises into solid carbonate rock in less than two years. Over 60% of injected CO₂ mineralises within four months . Researchers at the University of Edinburgh confirmed permanent mineralisation by tracking injected CO₂ using its natural chemical fingerprint, including carbon, water, and noble gas isotope signatures . The EU-funded Silverstone project aims to capture >95% of CO₂ from the Hellisheiði geothermal plant using Carbfix technology . Basalt, the ideal rock type for CO₂ mineral storage, is one of the most common rock types on Earth.

D.2 Olivine Enhanced Weathering Kinetics (2025)

Research published in 2025 investigated the viability of commercial olivine mixtures (purity ~55%, mean particle size ~39 µm) for enhanced weathering in seawater, quantifying dissolution kinetics, CO₂ sequestration capacity, and metal release profiles . A spatially explicit model of olivine-based marine enhanced rock weathering for the coastal zone of France was published by Geerts et al. (2025) in Environmental Research Letters, providing validated kinetic parameters at 1/24° × 1/24° (~15 km²) resolution .

Appendix E: Passive Daytime Radiative Cooling (PDRC) — Materials Benchmarks

Multiple 2025 publications validated PDRC performance of SiO₂/TiO₂/PDMS multilayer architectures:

· Sprayable superhydrophobic PDRC coatings using high-bandgap SiO₂ to mitigate UV absorption coupled with unmodified PDMS for high thermal emissivity and low surface energy were demonstrated with sub-ambient temperature regulation .
· Nested-bilayer daytime radiative cooling films featuring ZnS@SiO₂ photonic crystals and a TiO₂/PDMS bottom layer achieved robust full-colour performance .
· A systematic modelling framework based on optical admittance method and particle swarm optimisation was proposed for design and optimisation of PDRC multilayer thin-film structures .
· A sandwich-structured PDMS-based composite with hollow SiO₂ microspheres and rutile-TiO₂ microparticles achieved all-day sub-ambient radiative cooling .

These experimental results confirm that PDRC surface temperatures 3–8 °C below ambient, with net cooling power of 40–100 W m⁻², are achievable — the calibration range adopted for SST ENVI-met simulations (WP6).

Appendix F: EU Carbon Removal Certification Framework (CRCF) — Regulatory Status

The CRCF Regulation (EU) 2024/3012 entered into force in December 2024, establishing the EU's first voluntary framework for certifying carbon removals, carbon farming, and carbon storage in products. The framework sets broad certification criteria linked to Quantification, Additionality, Long-term storage, and SUstainability — the QU.A.L.ITY acronym .

Key milestones:

· 3 February 2026: The European Commission adopted the first certification methodology as a delegated regulation, covering three permanent carbon removal activities: Direct Air Carbon Capture and Sequestration (DACCS), Bioenergy with Carbon Capture and Storage (BECCS), and biochar .
· Publication and entry into force: The Delegated Act was published in the Official Journal and entered into force on 7 May 2026 .
· Certification bodies: As of April 2026, certification bodies were accepting applications under the new DACCS methodology.
· Post-2030 implementation package: A public consultation on the post-2030 implementation package closed in May 2026.
· Expert Group: At the 23 April 2026 Expert Group on Carbon Removals meeting, the Commission presented advancements in developing carbon farming certification methodologies, with legislative approval expected in summer 2026 .

