Appendix I: Lead-Free and Low-Lead Perovskite Technology

Multiple 2025 publications report advances in tin-based and lead-free perovskite devices:

· Simulated lead-free tin-based perovskite/silicon tandem design achieved 27.35% efficiency (SCAPS-1D) .
· Certified tin-based perovskite cell efficiency reached 17.89% (certified 17.71%) with stable operation exceeding 1,500 hours .
· A computational study in Scientific Reports demonstrated that a lead-free Cs₂TiI₆ bottom cell paired with low-lead CsPb₀.₇₅Sn₀.₂₅IBr₂ top cell achieved a simulated tandem efficiency of 33.39% with Voc of 2.04 V .
· FASnI₃-based lead-free perovskite cells achieved a PCE of 15.38% .

These results confirm that while lead-free tin-based tandems have not yet reached parity with lead-containing variants in experimental demonstrations, computational studies indicate headroom exceeding 27–33% — directly supporting the SST WP1 target of identifying ≥1 lead-free stack with simulated efficiency >28%.

Appendix J: Janus Membrane Fog Harvesting

A Janus fibrous membrane (PVDF-TrFE/PAN) with asymmetric wettability and surface potential was demonstrated in 2025 via sequential electrospinning: a superhydrophilic PAN fibre layer deposited first, followed by a hydrophobic PVDF-TrFE layer. The membrane facilitates all sequential processes required for effective fog collection — droplet capture, directional water transport, and rapid shedding — providing the bio-inspired design validated in the SST canopy architecture . The dual-wettability gradient generates Laplace pressure for spontaneous unidirectional droplet transport, matching the Namib beetle-inspired design concept referenced in the proposal.

Appendix K: Dielectric Elastomer Actuators for Solar Tracking

Jiang et al. (2025) demonstrated organic photovoltaic mini-modules providing over 5,000 V for energy autonomy of dielectric elastomer actuators (DEAs), published in Nature Communications 16, 2048 . DEAs, analogous to artificial muscles, require voltages exceeding 1,000 V, typically supplied by amplifiers with batteries or power grids. This organic PV-DEA integration validates the feasibility of self-powered, lightweight actuator systems for the phyllotactic leaflet tracking described in the SST canopy architecture.

